(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 12, part: `Part Four: The Chair`, title: `The Next Chair`, weeks: `Months 16 to 18`,
  tasks: [`L1`,`L4`,`L3`,`L23`,`L5`,`L11`,`L25`,`L7`,`L8`,`L21`,`L16`,`L13`,`L19`,`E1`,`E3`,`C5`,`SA7`,`SA9`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Wednesday, January 5, Maria opened a blank document and typed: Cascade Valley Health, Information Technology Strategic Plan, 2028 to 2030. Then she stopped.`,
    `The last one was in the shared drive. Ochoa had written it three years ago, 2025 to 2027, forty pages with his name on the cover. She read it at her desk with a cup of coffee going cold. Every initiative had a date beside it, and nearly every date had a check mark. MedaLink was on page thirty-one, in one line: assess options before end of support. Somebody had done that. It had taken her fifteen months.`,
    `She'd never written a CIO's plan. She'd never been in the room when one was written. And the board would read this one as Ochoa's, whoever typed it.`,
    `That afternoon she went down the hall to ask him where to start. His door was open and his chair was empty. The bookshelf behind his desk had gaps in it. Marcus had come up from the basement that morning carrying an interface engine manual from 1998. Ochoa had given it to him, and Marcus said it was wrong about everything and he was keeping it anyway.`,
    `Her phone buzzed on the walk back. Lucia. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"med surg starts monday. hospital, real patients. what do i bring"</span>`,
    `Maria typed the first true thing she thought of. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Two pens that work. A watch with a second hand. Eat before you go."</span> A minute later: <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"that's it?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"That's most of it."</span>`,
    `Back at her desk she wrote one question under the title, the one Ochoa had asked her in April about the business case. Who reads this in 2030? Then she sat looking at it for a long time, because she didn't know.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: the last page`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Start upstairs`,
        body: [`Sunday, January 9, Ochoa took the back booth at the Greeley diner and pushed the legal pad across the table to her side, pen and all. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"You write this time,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"I'll talk."</span>`,
          `Strategic planning starts above IT. The organization has a mission, why it exists, and a vision, where it wants to be. Before it sets goals, it scans its environment with a SWOT: strengths and weaknesses inside, opportunities and threats outside, like payers, regulation, the workforce, and attackers. Then it sets a few goals for three to five years. The IT strategic plan comes after, and it traces back to those goals one by one. That's strategic alignment. A project that can't name the goal it serves doesn't go in.`,
          `The plan itself has a shape. Where you are: a current state assessment of applications, infrastructure, people, spending, and risks. Where the organization's going. The initiatives that close the gap, on a roadmap by year. How decisions get made, which is governance. What it costs, capital and operating. How you'll know it worked, which is measures. And what could go wrong. It serves every kind of place the system runs: hospitals, a critical access hospital, clinics, home health, and any partner you host, like a community health center with its own board and its own patients.`],
        terms: [[`IT strategic plan`,`A multi-year plan, aligned to the organization's goals, covering current state, initiatives, roadmap, governance, cost, measures, and risks.`],[`Current state assessment`,`An inventory of today's applications, infrastructure, staff, spending, and risks, used as the starting point for the plan.`],[`Community health center`,`A health center, often federally funded, that provides primary care to anyone regardless of ability to pay, usually on a sliding fee scale, with its own board.`]],
        exam: `The IT plan derives from the organization's strategic plan, not from IT's backlog or a vendor's roadmap. What comes first is the organization's goals and a current state assessment. Strategic alignment means naming the goal each initiative serves.` },
      { h: `More asks than hands`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Everybody will want something in it,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Your job is to say what fits, and show why."</span>`,
          `Forecast first, from drivers: patient volumes, new services, users, and the plan's own initiatives, redone every year. Then manage everything as one portfolio, because every project draws on the same people and the same money. Mandatory work goes first: security, regulation, and anything losing vendor support. Rank the rest against the goals, risk, and value, and against capacity in analyst hours, not just dollars. Draw the line. Above it is funded and staffed. Below it gets a date or an honest no.`,
          `Each initiative comes in as a proposal with a benefits realization plan: an owner in the business, a baseline, a target, and review dates. Then show your work. Put the resources, the budget, and the ranking where a hospital president can see why her request sits below the line, and what would move it up.`],
        terms: [[`Portfolio management`,`Managing all projects together against shared people and money, ranked by strategy, risk, value, and capacity.`],[`Capacity`,`The staff time actually available for project work after support and operations are covered.`]],
        exam: `For competing requests, the answer is governance that ranks by strategic criteria and capacity, then publishes the result with reasons. To promote stakeholder understanding, show the resources, the budget, and where each request sits, openly.` },
      { h: `Numbers with a yardstick`,
        body: [`A key performance indicator, a KPI, is a measure tied to a goal, with a target, an owner, a data source, and a schedule. A service level agreement sets performance targets between a provider and its customer, inside the organization or out. A vendor SLA usually adds remedies. KPIs often measure SLAs. Pick a few of each.`,
          `Comparative analytics holds you against others. A benchmark is only fair if the definitions match and the peers look like you. Report where you fall, median or percentile, and treat the gap as a question to answer.`,
          `Then the money. Operating budgets pay for this year: salaries, subscriptions, support. Capital buys things that last. Budget variance is budget minus actual. For an expense line, a negative variance is unfavorable. Divide by the budget for the percent. Split one-time causes from recurring ones, and reforecast. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Glenn forgives a bad month,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"He doesn't forgive a surprise."</span>`],
        terms: [[`KPI`,`Key performance indicator. A measure tied to a goal, with a target, owner, data source, and reporting schedule.`],[`Benchmark`,`A comparison with peer organizations or a standard, valid only when definitions and peer groups match.`],[`Budget variance`,`Budget minus actual, in dollars and as a percent of budget. For expenses, negative is unfavorable.`]],
        exam: `Expect a variance calculation and a benchmark trap: check definitions and the peer group before acting on a gap. Judge a predictive model or a pilot on your own data, by site, against a matched baseline. An area under the curve of 0.5 is a coin flip and 1.0 is perfect.` },
      { h: `Write the jobs down`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"People are most of the plan,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"And the part nobody writes down."</span> A job description names a role's purpose, its duties, its decision rights, who it reports to, and the skills it needs. Write what the role doesn't own, too. For any role one person holds alone, write a succession plan: who could step in, how ready they are, and what training gets them there.`,
          `New roles belong in the plan when the work has outgrown the old ones. A chief nursing information officer leads nursing informatics the way the CMIO leads it for physicians. Policies carry the plan's rules. A policy says what and why, and governance approves it. A procedure says how. Each needs an owner and a review date.`],
        terms: [[`Job description`,`A written statement of a role's purpose, duties, decision rights, reporting line, and required competencies.`],[`Succession plan`,`A plan naming who could fill a critical role, how ready each is, and how they'll be developed.`]],
        exam: `For a single point of failure in a person, the answer is a written succession plan with named, trained backups, not a quick hire or an informal promise. Policies need governance approval and an owner, not an email.` },
      { h: `The room`,
        body: [`When two leaders both look at you in a meeting, don't pick a side. Name what they agree on, settle the criteria before the options, and decide by the method the room agreed to. In a board meeting the chair runs the room, and when the chair hands you the floor, the same rules hold. In front of a board, lead with the recommendation and the decision you need, show the risks, and brief the people who'll speak before the meeting starts.`],
        terms: [[`Facilitation`,`Running a meeting so the group reaches a decision fairly: shared goal first, criteria before options, an agreed way to decide.`]],
        exam: `In a conflict, the facilitator's first move is common ground and criteria, not a vote or a side. For a board, the recommendation comes first.` },
      { h: `Irene's card`,
        body: [`Ochoa paid, then sat back down. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"One more. This field changes faster than a nursing license does."</span> Keeping IT competencies current means continuing education, professional associations, conferences, and certifications that make you keep learning to renew them. For the team, it means a development plan for each person, with time and money behind it. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Kiran has a CPHIMS study guide in his drawer. Marcus told him to get one. Fund what they ask for."</span>`,
          `Maria had brought something too. It had come Saturday, in Irene's square handwriting, a card with a wren on the front. She slid it across the table.`,
          `<prop type="doc" title="A card from Irene Kowalski">Santos. You told me Sunday that Ray handed you his plan to write. Ray doesn't hand anybody anything by accident. Write it for the night shift. They're the ones who find out if it works. I renewed my critical care certification every three years until I retired, and every time I learned what I'd forgotten. Keep learning, and make your people do it too. Coffee's still bad. Come by. I.K.</prop>`,
          `Ochoa read it. He folded it along its crease and handed it back without a word about the second line. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Irene Kowalski,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"She always did read a room before she walked into it."</span> Then he tore off the page Maria had filled and gave her that too.`],
        terms: [[`Continuing education`,`Ongoing learning, often required to renew a license or certification, that keeps skills current.`]],
        exam: `To maintain competencies, choose ongoing education, certification with renewal requirements, and funded development plans over one-time training at go-live.` }
    ],
    video: [`VxIZHL6kB4E`,`What Is IT Strategy? [How to Create the Best Information Technology Strategy for Your Organization]`,`Digital Transformation with Eric Kimberling`,`9:59`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f12s1`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L4`, title:`A clean page`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, January 10, Maria had nine pages and no plan yet. Kiran had sent her a spreadsheet of every open request from every hospital, forty-four rows. Corvane had sent its product roadmap for the next three years, with a cover note from Brett. Aisha had sent a list titled What We Should Be Doing, and it was good.`,`Ochoa's old plan sat open in the other window. Three sources, and all of them were somebody's list. She could see a plan that was mostly a long table of projects with prices beside them. It would be done by Friday.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Turn Kiran's forty-four requests into a three-year schedule, with costs and dates.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`It's done by Friday, and it's a schedule. Glenn reads it and asks which of Cascade's goals request thirty-one serves. Nothing on the page says.`,
          why:`A list of requests is a backlog, not a strategy. An IT strategic plan starts from the organization's goals and an honest look at where you are, then decides which work closes the gap.`},
        {t:`Build it on Corvane's three-year roadmap, since the EHR is most of what IT runs.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The draft reads like Corvane's brochure with Cascade's logo on it. Nate notices there's nothing about the locks on a clinic in Rawlins.`,
          why:`A vendor's roadmap tells you what the vendor plans to sell. Your plan starts from your organization's goals and your own current state.`},
        {t:`Start from the system's goals and a current state check, then fit the work.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`She pulls Cascade's strategic plan, five goals, and writes each across the top of a page. Then a current state assessment, one page per area: applications, infrastructure, people, spending, risks. Rawlins is in it twice, once for the network and once for the locks. Only then does she open Kiran's spreadsheet, and each of his forty-four rows gets the goal it serves, or a blank. Eleven rows stay blank. Kiran reads them and laughs a little. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Four of those are mine."</span>`,
          why:`An IT strategic plan derives from the organization's strategy. Start with the goals and a current state assessment, then fill in initiatives, a roadmap, governance, costs, measures, and risks. Requests that can't name a goal get questioned.`},
        {t:`Update Ochoa's 2025 plan with new dates, since its structure already worked.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The structure holds up. But the 2025 plan was written before One Chart, before Rawlins, and before anybody in Wyoming had a portal. The new dates sit on old assumptions.`,
          why:`Reusing a proven structure is fine. A plan still has to start from today's goals and today's current state, and a lot changed in three years.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s2`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E1`, title:`Across town`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, January 13, the chief executive of Snowy Range Community Health called from Laramie. Her health center wasn't part of Cascade. It ran six clinics in two counties, saw anyone who walked in, and charged on a sliding scale by income. About a third of its patients had no insurance. Its old EHR vendor had just been bought, and the price had doubled.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Half our patients end up at your hospitals,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"We'd like to come onto your Corvane. Corvane says big customers can host small ones. Could you put us in your plan?"</span>`,`Kiran was already thinking about the build. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"We have forty clinics on Corvane. It'd be forty-six. Same build."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Treat Snowy Range as six more Cascade clinics, using the same build and policies.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The build goes fast. Then Snowy Range's billing manager asks where the sliding fee scale is. Its board asks who decided its privacy policies. Neither answer is Snowy Range.`,
          why:`A community health center isn't a hospital clinic. It has its own board, its own patients' rights, sliding fee billing, and its own federal reporting. Plan for the organization it is.`},
        {t:`Plan it as its own organization, with a hosting agreement, a BAA, and its own needs.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria puts it in the plan's second year as a hosted partner, with its own line. A hosting agreement and a business associate agreement, written with Joan. Its own records and access, walled off from Cascade's except where a patient's care crosses over. Sliding fee billing and its federal grant reports in the build. A seat at the clinical informatics council. The chief executive reads the draft and says it's the first time a hospital system asked what her patients needed instead of what her clinics cost.`,
          why:`Know the type of organization. A community health center serves patients regardless of ability to pay, often on a sliding fee scale, with its own board and federal funding rules. Hosting it means a separate organization with its own agreements, privacy boundaries, and needs.`},
        {t:`Decline, since Snowy Range isn't Cascade and its patients aren't Cascade's job.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Snowy Range signs with the vendor that doubled its price. Its patients keep arriving at Laramie's ED with records nobody can see.`,
          why:`Not every request belongs in the plan, but this one serves Cascade's own goals: shared patients and safer transitions. Decline for a reason, not because it's someone else's building.`},
        {t:`Add it to year three, and work out the details once One Chart has settled.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's a reasonable place on the roadmap. But year three with no details is a promise nobody has priced, and Snowy Range's contract ends in eighteen months.`,
          why:`Sequencing is fair. Putting a partner in the plan without knowing what kind of organization it is, or when it needs you, leaves the hard part for later.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s3`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E3`, title:`The nurse's chair`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, January 18, the three Wyoming CNOs and the system's chief nursing officer read the people section of Maria's draft. Gail had circled one line in pencil. Nursing informatics: covered by the vice president of clinical systems.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Gail">"That's you,"</span> Gail said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"You've been our nursing informatics officer for fifteen months, and it isn't anybody's job. Aisha has a title for the doctors. We don't."</span> She let that sit. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Denise Harmon asked me last week whether anybody was ever going to write that job."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep nursing informatics with clinical systems, and revisit it after year one.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The CNOs accept it. It works as long as the vice president of clinical systems is a nurse who goes to every unit, and nothing in the plan says she will be.`,
          why:`What works now isn't a role. If a job depends on one person's background, write the role so it outlasts her.`},
        {t:`Fold nursing into Aisha's role, so one leader owns all clinical informatics.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Aisha is generous about it and has no time for it. Nursing documentation questions wait behind sepsis, and the CNOs notice.`,
          why:`The CMIO leads physician informatics. Nursing is most of the users and most of the documentation, and it needs a nurse leader of its own.`},
        {t:`Make it an IT director post in clinical systems that builds nursing content.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The director builds well. Nursing content still has no nurse executive deciding what goes in it, and the CNOs keep calling Maria.`,
          why:`Building content is IT's job. Owning nursing practice in the record is a nursing leader's. Don't confuse the custodian with the owner.`},
        {t:`Create a CNIO: a nurse leader who owns nursing informatics, peer to the CMIO.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`She writes it into the plan's first year. A chief nursing information officer, reporting to the system CNO with a dotted line to the CIO. Co-chair of the council with Aisha. Owns nursing documentation, BCMA practice, and nurse adoption. She wrote it knowing it was the part of the job she'd miss, which was how she knew it had to be somebody's whole job. IT builds what that role and the council decide. Gail reads it and taps the dotted line. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"That's the part that makes it real."</span>`,
          why:`Know the HIMS roles. The CNIO leads nursing informatics the way the CMIO leads it for physicians: nursing's voice in design, content, and adoption. IT is the builder. Write the role so it doesn't depend on who's in it now.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s4`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L25`, title:`Over`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, January 20, Glenn's year-end report came in at 7:02, the way his emails always did.`,
        `<prop type="email" from="Glenn Morrow" to="Maria Santos" time="7:02 AM" subject="Clinical systems, 2027">Maria, your 2027 operating budget was $18.4 million. Actual was $19.55 million. The board's finance committee will see this line in March, the same month it sees your plan. I'd like to understand it before they do. Glenn</prop>`,
        ((G.softroi)
          ?`He'd added a line under his name. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"I still have the first draft of the business case. I read your numbers twice."</span>`
          :`He'd added a line under his name. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"Your business case held. Let's see if your budget did."</span>`),
        `Kiran had the drivers by noon. Go-live overtime and superuser backfill, $820,000. The ransomware response and the insurance deductible, $250,000. The second Rawlins circuit, started early, $180,000 for the year. Cascade's MedaLink support contract, no longer needed after cutover, ended a month early, which saved $100,000.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Show each driver, split one-time from recurring, and reforecast 2028.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:3},
          after:`One page. The total, 6.25 percent over. Then each driver, marked one-time or recurring. The go-live, the ransomware, and MedaLink's early end were one-time, $970,000 net. The circuit recurs, $180,000 a year that the 2028 budget never had. She adds it to the 2028 reforecast with a line about why. Glenn reads it at his desk. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"So what recurs is a hundred and eighty thousand."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"And it's in next year's budget now."</span>`,
          why:`Explain a variance by its drivers. Budget minus actual gives the size. Splitting one-time from recurring tells finance what will happen again, and the reforecast puts recurring costs where they belong. No surprises.`},
        {t:`Report the total, and explain that it was all from go-live, which is over.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Glenn accepts most of it. Then he asks about the circuit, which isn't from go-live and isn't over, and the answer isn't on the page.`,
          why:`Most of it was one-time, which is worth saying. Rolling every driver into one story hides the recurring cost that will be back next year.`},
        {t:`Cover the overrun by quietly deferring a 2028 project, so the total looks right.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The 2028 numbers balance. In June the deferred project's owner asks Glenn why it vanished, and Glenn finds out what it paid for.`,
          why:`Hiding a variance by moving money moves the risk out of sight. Explain the overrun, and change scope in the open if you have to.`},
        {t:`Point out the overrun came from vendor and insurer costs outside her control.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`Glenn agrees some of it was outside her control. He also notes that the overtime was hers, and so is the budget.`,
          why:`A variance isn't a question of blame. Explain the causes, own the line, and say what changes.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s5`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L7`, title:`Forty-four`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, January 25, the forty-four requests came to the IT steering committee. Together they needed about 61,000 analyst hours over three years. After support and upgrades, Maria's team had about 38,000.`,`Each hospital president had sent one email about their own request. The Fort Collins president had sent two. Aisha's predictive program was on the list, and so was Nate's security work from Rawlins, and so was Snowy Range.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rank all forty-four in steering by goal, risk, and hours, then publish it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Mandatory work goes first: Nate's security list and two regulatory changes. The rest get scored against the five goals, risk, and value, then stacked against the 38,000 hours. Twenty-six land above the line. Eighteen land below it, each with a date to come back or a plain no and the reason. Maria sends every hospital president the whole list, not just their own row. The Fort Collins president calls, annoyed. Then he asks what would move his request up, and she tells him.`,
          why:`Manage projects as one portfolio, and make it understandable. Put mandatory work first, rank the rest against strategy, risk, and value, and fit them to real capacity. Then publish the whole ranking, the hours, and the reasons, so every leader can see where they stand and what would change it.`},
        {t:`Fund the mandatory items, then let each hospital pick one of its own requests.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Every president gets something, which keeps the peace. Rawlins picks a small fix, Fort Collins picks a big one, and nobody asks which ones serve the system's goals.`,
          why:`Protecting mandatory work is right. Giving each site a pick is fair to sites, not to the strategy. Rank the whole portfolio together.`},
        {t:`Put all forty-four requests in the plan, spread evenly across the three years.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The plan promises 61,000 hours of work to a team with 38,000. By summer every project is late, and Kiran is staying late again.`,
          why:`A plan that ignores capacity isn't a plan. It's overtime. Fit the work to the people you have, and say no to the rest.`},
        {t:`Take the ten biggest requests by cost, since those matter most to the board.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The ten biggest include a data center refresh and an imaging archive. They leave out the small fixes nurses asked for, which cost little and matter at 3 AM.`,
          why:`Size isn't value. Rank by strategy, risk, and value against capacity, and small work often wins.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s6`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L11`, title:`Peers`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, February 3, two days after Maria sent Ochoa her draft, Glenn's analyst brought a benchmark. A national survey of health systems put IT operating spending at a median of 3.1 percent of revenue. Cascade spent $46.8 million on $1.8 billion, 2.6 percent.`,`Aisha saw it before lunch. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Put it on page one,"</span> she wrote. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"We're nine million under our peers. That pays for my whole program."</span>`,`Tasha had sent one too. The service desk fixed 70 percent of calls on the first contact. The survey's median was 78.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use the gap to ask the board for $9 million, since peers spend that much more.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`Glenn's analyst reads the survey's appendix that afternoon. Its peers include academic centers with research computing in their IT budgets. The nine million shrinks before the board sees it, and so does Maria's credibility.`,
          why:`A benchmark gap is a question, not a budget request. Check the definitions and the peer group before you act on it.`},
        {t:`Show it on page one, with a footnote saying definitions may differ.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The footnote is honest. The board reads the headline and the footnote gets lost, which a footnote usually does.`,
          why:`Disclosing limits is right. If you know the comparison may not match, check it before it goes on page one.`},
        {t:`Leave benchmarks out, since no two health systems are really alike.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The plan has no comparison at all. A board member brings his own article in March, and it's worse than the survey.`,
          why:`Comparisons are hard, not useless. Find a fair peer group and use it carefully.`},
        {t:`Check its definitions and peers first, then use it beside outcomes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Glenn's analyst rebuilds the peer group: multi-hospital systems with rural sites and no medical school, on the same definition of IT spending. The median drops to 2.8. Cascade is 0.2 points under, not 0.5. Tasha checks the desk number the same way. The survey counts password resets the phone system fixes on its own, and Cascade doesn't. Matched, the desk median is 74, so Cascade's 70 is a real gap, just a smaller one. The plan shows both gaps with their definitions, beside the outcomes. Aisha sighs. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Three point six million is still three point six million."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"And Glenn will believe it."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative analytics only works when definitions match and peers are truly comparable. Check both, report where you fall, and treat the gap as a question to explain, next to outcomes, before you use it to argue for money.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s7`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`Silent`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, February 8, Aisha brought the Sepsis Predict results to Maria's office. Corvane's model had run silently since the April upgrade at the eight Corvane hospitals, and since October in Wyoming, scoring every patient and showing no one.`,`Corvane's brochure said its area under the curve was 0.87. Kiran had checked it against the residents' chart reviews, hospital by hospital. Greeley, 0.85. Fort Collins, 0.83. Cheyenne, 0.79. Rawlins, 0.68, on nineteen septic patients since October.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Overall it's 0.81,"</span> Aisha said. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"I want it live in all eleven on April first. It's the first thing in my section of the plan."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Turn it on everywhere April first, since 0.81 overall is a strong model.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`It goes live in April. At Rawlins it flags the wrong patients often enough that nurses stop trusting it by May, and they stop trusting Aisha's other alerts with it.`,
          why:`An overall number hides how a model does at each site. Judge it where it'll be used. At 0.68 it isn't ready for Rawlins.`},
        {t:`Use Corvane's 0.87 and its default threshold, since it's the vendor's model.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The vendor's threshold was set on other hospitals' patients. At Greeley it fires twice as often as Kiran's numbers said it would.`,
          why:`A vendor's validation describes the vendor's sites. Set thresholds and judge performance on your own data.`},
        {t:`Keep it silent at all eleven until every site has a full year of data.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Nothing goes wrong. Greeley and Fort Collins, where it works, wait another year for a tool that would have helped.`,
          why:`Caution at the weak sites is right. Holding back the sites where it's proven delays a benefit for no reason.`},
        {t:`Go live where it's proven, keep it silent elsewhere, and recheck by site.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`Greeley and Fort Collins go live in April, with a threshold Kiran and Aisha pick from Cascade's own two by two. Cheyenne stays silent until it has more cases. Rawlins stays silent, and Aisha reads the nineteen charts to find out why. Every quarter, a named owner checks it by site. Aisha takes the plan line back and rewrites it herself. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Two sites, then earn the rest."</span>`,
          why:`Interpret a predictive model on local data, site by site. Go live where it performs, set thresholds from your own results, keep it silent where it doesn't, and monitor for drift with an owner.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s8`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L8`, title:`Pasted`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, February 14, Joan called at 8:40. A first-year resident had asked her, politely, whether it was all right to paste patient notes into a free AI chatbot on his phone to draft discharge summaries. He'd been doing it for a month. So had three other residents.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Joan">"He asked,"</span> Joan said. <span class="said" data-who="Joan">"That's the good news. We have no policy that says no, and nothing in your plan that says what yes looks like."</span>`,
        ((G.sharedlogin)
          ?`Nate was on the line too. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"It's the shared login in the Rawlins ED again,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"People take the fast way when we don't give them a safe one."</span>`
          :`Nate was on the line too. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"Penwright's AI is approved, with a business associate agreement,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"This one isn't. They can't tell the difference from the screen."</span>`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Email every clinician today that AI tools are banned, and call that the policy.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The email goes out at noon. By March it's in four thousand inboxes and no policy library, and residents are using a different chatbot.`,
          why:`An email isn't a policy. It has no owner, no approval, and no review date, and a ban with no approved option pushes people to the next tool.`},
        {t:`Have Nate block AI websites at the firewall, and leave it at that.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The sites are blocked on Cascade's network by afternoon. The residents use their phones on cellular, which no firewall at Cascade can see.`,
          why:`A technical block is a useful control. Without a policy that says what's allowed and why, people route around it.`},
        {t:`Let each department write its own AI rules, since the uses all differ.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`Radiology writes one. Hospital medicine writes a different one. Nursing doesn't write one. Nobody's sure which applies to a resident on a nursing unit.`,
          why:`Departments can add detail, but privacy and security rules need one owner and one policy for the organization.`},
        {t:`Let Joan assess it, and take an AI policy through governance with an owner.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`Before noon, Aisha tells the four residents to stop today, and why. Joan runs her assessment on their month. Maria drafts the policy with Joan, Nate, Aisha, and legal. Approved AI tools only, each with a BAA. No patient information in public ones. New AI tools come through the technology intake. Training for every clinician, an owner, and a review in a year. It goes to the policy committee in March, and into the plan as its own line. The resident who asked helps write the training.`,
          why:`Contain first: the disclosures stop today. Then policies need governance: an owner, the right approvers, a findable home, training, and a review date. A privacy event goes to the privacy officer. A good policy also names the safe option, so people don't need a workaround.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s9`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L16`, title:`Kiran's page`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, February 17, Maria wrote Kiran's job down. The plan needed it. Every year of the roadmap leaned on the same two things: clinical content that stayed current, and superusers who could teach. Kiran had been doing both since October, on top of everything else, and nobody had written any of it down.`,
        `Halfway down the page she stopped at the line for skills. Year two brought Meadowlark's patch data and other outside tools into the chart through FHIR. Years two and three took Sepsis Predict to the other hospitals, and after Monday's call about the residents and the chatbot, somebody had to be able to judge every AI tool that came in. The last class anyone on her team had taken was Corvane's build training for One Chart. Most of what they knew about FHIR, they'd learned from Marcus at a whiteboard.`,
        `She asked Kiran to her office at four, so he'd have to leave at five. He came in with a notebook, as if it were a meeting about somebody else. When she showed him the skills line, he nodded. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"I've been reading about the sepsis model at night,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"I can keep up."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Send Kiran and two of the analysts to Corvane's user conference every single year.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`They go in May and come back excited about features Cascade already owns. Nobody's skills plan changes, and the FHIR work in year two still lands on Marcus's whiteboard.`,
          why:`A conference gives exposure, and that's worth something. Keeping competencies current takes a plan tied to the roadmap: the gaps named, the learning scheduled, and a follow-up.`},
        {t:`Map the plan's skills to each role, find the gaps, and fund a plan per person.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:7,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`They build it together before five. Maria lists what the plan needs by year: FHIR apps, model validation, AI tool review, and the content work. Kiran rates himself and the team against it, honestly, on his third try. Each person gets a development plan with four protected hours a week on the schedule, a paid certification where the role needs one, and a review every January. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Not at night,"</span> Maria says. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Tuesday afternoons, on the schedule, where I can see it."</span> Then the role page itself. Clinical content and superuser program lead. He runs the council's content process and the train the trainer program. He doesn't own clinical content, the clinicians do. He isn't on call; the team rotates. Yesenia, the analyst Marcus taught first, is his deputy, and she shadows him on content until she can cover it. The skills the role keeps current are on the page, with the hours to learn them. Kiran reads to the bottom. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, surprised, almost laughing.">"You wrote my hours into a job description."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Somebody had to."</span>`,
          why:`Maintaining competencies starts from the strategy. Name the skills the plan will need, assess each person against them, and fund a development plan with protected time, certification where it matters, cross-training, and a yearly review. Learning that only happens at go-live or after midnight doesn't keep a team current.`},
        {t:`Bring in contractors who already know the new tools as each project gets started.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The contractors are good. When the sepsis rollout ends they leave, and so does everything they knew. The next project hires them again.`,
          why:`Buying skills one project at a time leaves the organization no more capable than before. Build the competencies the plan needs in your own people.`},
        {t:`Encourage study on their own time, and pay back any certification they pass.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Kiran says yes before she finishes the sentence. By April he's studying at midnight again, and Maria recognizes the schedule.`,
          why:`Unpaid study on personal time turns development into night work and wears out the people you most need. Put it on the schedule, with money behind it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s10`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L21`, title:`Good binder`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, February 22, Marcus came up from the basement at seven with a binder under his arm. Four inches thick, tabbed in three colors, RELAY on the spine in his block capitals. He set it on Maria's desk.`,
        ((G.blamedmarcus)
          ?`He didn't sit. Things between them had been polite since the readout in Laramie, and no warmer. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Flat, careful.">"I'm doing this for Relay,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Not for anybody in particular."</span>`
          :`He sat down without being asked, which he did now. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Gruff, dry.">"Good binder,"</span> he said. It was what he'd said about the one in Omaha.`),
        `Then he told her. His granddaughter in Tucson had a brother now. He wanted four days a week starting in July, and to be done for good at the end of 2030. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Your plan says Relay runs through 2030,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Somebody has to run it after. I want it in writing who. And I want it in writing that I'm here till I say I'm not."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Post his job now, so a new engineer can shadow him before July.`, s:0, d:{trust:-6,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The posting goes up Friday. Marcus sees it on the intranet before Maria tells him. It's Omaha with a nicer font.`,
          why:`Recruiting his replacement behind him confirms his oldest fear. Succession planning is done with the person, and it starts with the people you've already trained.`},
        {t:`Name the analyst he trained as his successor, and give her Relay in July.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She's good, and she's ready for some of it. In July she's the only one who can run Relay on Fridays, which is the problem Marcus was solving in the first place.`,
          why:`Naming a successor is right. One successor, handed everything at once, just moves the single point of failure. Name two, and stage it.`},
        {t:`Write a plan with him: two named successors, skill dates, and his role to 2030.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:7,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They write it together that morning. Two named successors: the other analyst he's been teaching, and a second he picks himself. Every Relay skill in his binder gets a sign-off date for each of them, the hardest last. From July he's principal interface engineer, four days a week, teaching, through December 2030, and the last line says the date moves only if he moves it. Maria signs it. Marcus reads the last line again. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Low, rough, almost smiling.">"This in my file?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It's in the plan."</span>`,
          why:`For a critical role held by one person, write a succession plan: named successors, readiness for each skill, training to close the gaps, and the incumbent's role in writing. Build it with the person, so passing on what he knows is his job, not his exit.`},
        {t:`Keep it informal, since he's earned her trust and a plan would feel cold.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`Nothing changes on paper. In 2030 nobody can say which skills the successors have, and Marcus's binder is the only plan Cascade has.`,
          why:`Trust doesn't replace a plan. Writing it down protects him and the hospitals at the same time.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s11`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`Practice`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, March 7, at 7:30, Ochoa didn't have a legal pad. He had her plan, eighty pages, with his initials on every one.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"I'm retiring,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"March thirty-first. I told the board chair in July. On Friday I'm going to tell the rest of them, and I'm going to recommend you as interim CIO. They'll decide. Not me."</span>`,
        `Maria didn't say anything for a while. She thought about the go or no-go meeting, and the command center in Cheyenne, and the exit conference at Greeley a year and a half ago. Every time, he'd taken a chair along the wall. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"You sat along the wall all year,"</span> she said.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Dry, quiet, almost smiling.">"I told you I'd be furniture,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Dry, quiet, almost smiling.">"I didn't say it was practice."</span> Then, plainer: <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"If I'd told you in October, you'd have run go-live for me. You ran it for Rawlins. I needed to know you would."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, rueful.">"Irene told me in January,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, rueful.">"I didn't hear it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Dry, warm.">"That's what Irene's for."</span> She looked at his initials, one on every page. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, honest.">"Who do I call at two in the morning?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, sure.">"Nobody. You built it so nobody has to."</span>`,
        `Wednesday she drove to Greeley to see Bev's pilot before Friday. The remote station sat at the charge desk on the fourth floor, where the fax machine used to be: two screens, two Rawlins beds, a headset. Bev was in the chair. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Dry, fond.">"Kid. You look like you're about to take report."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet.">"Ochoa's retiring."</span> Bev slid the headset down around her neck and looked at her a long moment. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Plain, sure.">"Then take it."</span>`,
        `Gail wanted Bev's numbers in Friday's plan. Five weeks, twenty-six patients watched. Five went to Greeley. In the same five weeks last year, twelve of twenty-nine similar patients had made the three-hour ride. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Transfers cut by more than half,"</span> Gail had written. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Put that on the slide."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put Gail's line on the slide, since the drop from 41 percent to 19 is large.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The board likes it. By summer, with ninety patients counted, the drop is a third, not half, and a board member asks what happened.`,
          why:`Percentages on twenty-six patients swing hard on one or two cases. Early numbers this small can't carry a claim that big.`},
        {t:`Show counts beside last year's, call it early, and judge it at six months.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`The slide says five of twenty-six this year, twelve of twenty-nine last year, five weeks in, too early to call. Beside it are the balancing measures. One patient kept at Rawlins had to transfer the next morning. Nights at both units are surveyed too. The six-month review date sits at the bottom. Bev reads it over Maria's shoulder. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"That's how I'd chart it."</span> Gail, on the phone, says fine, and that she'll say promising.`,
          why:`Interpret small-number outcomes honestly. Show counts, not just percents, against a matched baseline. Say it's early, add balancing measures for the risks, and judge it at the review the pilot set in advance.`},
        {t:`Leave the pilot out of the plan, since five weeks can't show anything yet.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The plan stays safe and says nothing. The board doesn't learn a pilot exists that might keep Rawlins patients home.`,
          why:`Caution is fair. Early data can still be reported if it's labeled early, with counts and a review date.`},
        {t:`Report only the transfers avoided, seven patients kept at home in five weeks.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Seven sounds concrete. It assumes every patient who stayed would have gone, and it leaves out the one who had to go the next day.`,
          why:`A count of benefits without a comparison and without the harms isn't an outcome. Show both sides against a baseline.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f12s12`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L19`, title:`Both of them`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, March 10, the board met at two on the sixth floor. Ochoa spoke first, for four minutes. He was retiring at the end of the month. He recommended Maria Santos as interim CIO, and the board would take it up in executive session after the plan. Then he went and sat along the wall.`,`Maria led with the ask: approve the plan, and $3 million in new capital for its first year. Twenty minutes in, she reached the capital page, and the room split.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha" data-tone="Firm, heated, controlled.">"Patients die of sepsis, not firewalls,"</span> Aisha said. She wanted $2.2 million: Sepsis Predict, Bev's remote ICU at Laramie, and Meadowlark for four hundred patients. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Dry, flat, firm.">"A clinic in Rawlins was closed for a week,"</span> Glenn said. He wanted $2.4 million for Nate's security list first. Together that was $4.6 million. Every face at the table turned to Maria.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Back Glenn's security list, since the ransomware is the board's freshest worry.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The board nods along. Aisha doesn't say another word in the meeting, and the predictive program loses a year and its loudest champion.`,
          why:`A facilitator who picks a side ends the discussion and loses half the room. Find what they share first.`},
        {t:`Split the $3 million evenly between Aisha's programs and Glenn's list.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`Each gets $1.5 million, and neither plan works at that size. Glenn calls it an average, which from Glenn isn't a compliment.`,
          why:`Splitting the difference skips the criteria. The goal is a decision on the merits, not an equal share.`},
        {t:`Name the shared goal, agree on criteria, then stage the money behind gates.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"You're both talking about the same thing,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"The night nobody's watching."</span> Then she asks them for the criteria, not the money: harm prevented, how sure we are, and when we'd know. Both write theirs on the same page. Measured that way, the plan is $1.6 million for the security items Nate rates high, now. $200,000 to take Sepsis Predict live at Greeley and Fort Collins. $1.2 million for the remote ICU and Meadowlark, released in September if the pilots meet the measures they set in advance. Glenn checks the sum. Aisha checks the gate. The board votes yes.`,
          why:`Present a recommendation the board can test. Start from the shared goal, agree on the criteria before the options, and show how the money follows the evidence. Staged funding with gates lets data settle what argument can't, and the board decides on the merits, not on who argued louder.`},
        {t:`Ask the board to send the capital question to its finance committee next month.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It cools the room. The finance committee is Glenn's committee, and Aisha says so in the hallway. The plan starts its first year without its capital.`,
          why:`Deferring can help when the facts aren't ready. Here the facts were ready and both people were in the room. Facilitate it there.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f12s4`, title:`Operating budget variance`, setup:[`Clinical systems, 2027. The operating budget was $18,400,000 and actual spending was $19,550,000. Work in thousands of dollars.`,`Budget variance = budget minus actual. Variance percent = variance / budget. For an expense budget, a negative variance is unfavorable. Round percents to two decimals.`],
      table:[[`Driver`,`$K`,`Kind`],[`Go-live overtime`,`820`,`One-time`],[`Ransomware costs`,`250`,`One-time`],[`Rawlins circuit`,`180`,`Recurring`],[`MedaLink ended early`,`minus 100`,`One-time`]],
      fields:[{label:`Variance, thousands`, answer:-1150, tol:0},{label:`Variance, percent of budget`, answer:-6.25, tol:0.01},{label:`One-time drivers, net, thousands`, answer:970, tol:0},{label:`Recurring drivers, thousands`, answer:180, tol:0},{label:`Variance percent without one-time drivers`, answer:-0.98, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`Variance: 18,400 minus 19,550 = minus 1,150, or $1.15 million unfavorable.`,`Percent: minus 1,150 / 18,400 = minus 0.0625, or minus 6.25 percent.`,`One-time, net: 820 + 250 minus 100 = 970. Support that ends a month early saves that month once, so it's one-time too.`,`Recurring: the circuit, 180. That's what will come back in 2028 unless the budget plans for it.`,`Without one-time drivers, actual would have been 19,550 minus 970 = 18,580. Variance 18,400 minus 18,580 = minus 180. Minus 180 / 18,400 = minus 0.00978, or minus 0.98 percent.`,`A 6.25 percent overrun that's mostly one-time is a different conversation with the CFO than a 6.25 percent overrun that recurs. Split them before the meeting.`] },
    { after:`f12s6`, title:`KPIs against a benchmark`, setup:[`Cascade spent $46.8 million on IT operations on $1.8 billion of revenue. The national survey's median is 3.1 percent of revenue. Rebuilt with comparable peers and the same definitions, the median is 2.8 percent.`,`The service desk took 6,400 calls in a month and fixed 4,480 on the first contact. With automated password resets taken out to match Cascade's definition, the desk benchmark median is 74 percent.`,`IT spend percent = IT spending / revenue. Gap = Cascade's value minus the benchmark. Round percents to two decimals and dollars to the nearest tenth of a million.`],
      fields:[{label:`IT spending, percent of revenue`, answer:2.6, tol:0.01},{label:`Gap to the published median, points`, answer:-0.5, tol:0.01},{label:`Gap to the comparable median, points`, answer:-0.2, tol:0.01},{label:`Dollars to reach the comparable median, millions`, answer:3.6, tol:0.05},{label:`First-contact resolution, percent`, answer:70, tol:0.01},{label:`More first-contact fixes a month to reach 74 percent`, answer:256, tol:0}],
      solution:[`IT spend: 46.8 / 1,800 = 0.026, or 2.60 percent.`,`Gap to the published median: 2.60 minus 3.10 = minus 0.50 points.`,`Gap to the comparable median: 2.60 minus 2.80 = minus 0.20 points.`,`Spending at the comparable median: 0.028 x 1,800 = 50.4 million. 50.4 minus 46.8 = 3.6 million. The headline survey would have said 0.031 x 1,800 = 55.8, a gap of 9.0 million, mostly from a different peer group.`,`First-contact resolution: 4,480 / 6,400 = 0.70, or 70.00 percent.`,`To reach 74 percent: 0.74 x 6,400 = 4,736. 4,736 minus 4,480 = 256 more a month, once the definitions match.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f12s1`, title:`Which part of the plan?`, intro:`An IT strategic plan has a shape, and the exam expects you to know its parts. Pick where each item belongs.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`An inventory of every application, its owner, its age, and its support status.`, choices:[`Current state assessment`,`Alignment to goals`,`Initiatives and roadmap`,`Governance`,`Financial plan`,`Performance measures`], answer:0, why:`Knowing what you have today, including what's losing support, is the current state assessment.`},
      {prompt:`A table showing which of the system's five goals each initiative serves.`, choices:[`Current state assessment`,`Alignment to goals`,`Initiatives and roadmap`,`Governance`,`Financial plan`,`Performance measures`], answer:1, why:`Tracing each initiative to an organizational goal is strategic alignment.`},
      {prompt:`Year one: CNIO hired and Sepsis Predict live at two sites. Year two: host Snowy Range.`, choices:[`Current state assessment`,`Alignment to goals`,`Initiatives and roadmap`,`Governance`,`Financial plan`,`Performance measures`], answer:2, why:`What gets done, and in what order across the years, is the roadmap.`},
      {prompt:`The IT steering committee ranks new requests quarterly against the plan's criteria.`, choices:[`Current state assessment`,`Alignment to goals`,`Initiatives and roadmap`,`Governance`,`Financial plan`,`Performance measures`], answer:3, why:`Who decides, and how, is governance.`},
      {prompt:`Capital and operating costs by year, with the recurring costs each initiative adds.`, choices:[`Current state assessment`,`Alignment to goals`,`Initiatives and roadmap`,`Governance`,`Financial plan`,`Performance measures`], answer:4, why:`The money, capital and operating, by year, is the financial plan.`},
      {prompt:`First-contact resolution at 74 percent by 2029, reported monthly by the service desk manager.`, choices:[`Current state assessment`,`Alignment to goals`,`Initiatives and roadmap`,`Governance`,`Financial plan`,`Performance measures`], answer:5, why:`A KPI with a target, an owner, and a schedule is a performance measure.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which document should a health system's IT strategic plan be derived from FIRST?`, opts:[`The organization's strategic plan`,`The EHR vendor's product roadmap`,`Last year's IT capital budget`,`The IT department's request backlog`], a:0, why:`The IT plan traces to the organization's mission, vision, and goals. Vendor roadmaps, budgets, and backlogs are inputs, not the starting point.`, task:`L4`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A draft IT strategic plan lists thirty projects with costs and dates, and nothing else. What is it MOST missing?`, opts:[`A technical design for each of the projects`,`Vendor quotes for each of the thirty projects`,`Current state assessment, governance, measures`,`A longer list of the requests that didn't fit`], a:2, why:`A strategic plan includes the current state, alignment to goals, a roadmap, governance, costs, measures, and risks. A project list is only the roadmap.`, task:`L4`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Hospital presidents complain that their requests were left out of the IT plan. Which response BEST builds their understanding?`, opts:[`Add one request from each hospital to keep peace`,`Publish the full ranking, criteria, and capacity`,`Send each president a note about their own request`,`Explain that the steering committee's vote is final`], a:1, why:`Promoting stakeholder understanding means showing the resources, the budget, and the ranking openly, with what would move a request up. A private note or a final word doesn't explain anything.`, task:`L7`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A CNO asks why IT can't start her unit's project this quarter. What should the IT leader show her?`, opts:[`Analyst capacity and where it ranks`,`The vendor's estimate for the work`,`A copy of IT's operating budget`,`The help desk ticket volume trend`], a:0, why:`Show the constraint and the priority: how much staff time exists, what's ahead of her project and why, and what would change its place. That's how stakeholders understand resources and prioritization.`, task:`L7`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Before acting on a survey showing the organization spends less on IT than its peers, what should the leader check FIRST?`, opts:[`Whether the board has already seen the survey`,`Whether the gap would cover a budget request`,`Whether the survey was published this year`,`Whether definitions and peer group match`], a:3, why:`A benchmark is only fair when the measure is defined the same way and the peers are comparable. Check both before using the gap.`, task:`L11`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A system spends $36 million on IT on $1.2 billion of revenue. The comparable peer median is 3.4 percent. How does it compare?`, opts:[`0.4 points above the median`,`3.4 points below the median`,`0.4 points below the median`,`11.8 points below the median`], a:2, why:`36 / 1,200 = 0.03, or 3.0 percent. 3.0 minus 3.4 = minus 0.4 points, so 0.4 points below. 11.8 is the gap as a percent of the median (0.4 / 3.4), not points.`, task:`L11`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which approach BEST maintains an IT team's competencies over time?`, opts:[`Vendor training at each go-live`,`A yearly reminder to read manuals`,`A funded plan for each person`,`Hiring new staff when skills lag`], a:2, why:`Competencies stay current through ongoing development: a plan for each person with time and money for continuing education, conferences, and certifications that require renewal.`, task:`L16`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`An IT leader's analysts were last trained on the EHR at go-live four years ago. What should the leader do?`, opts:[`Wait for the next major upgrade to retrain them`,`Ask the vendor to send its standard user manual`,`Have each analyst train themselves as needed`,`Set development plans with education and sign-offs`], a:3, why:`Maintaining competencies is ongoing: development plans, continuing education, release-note reviews, and skill sign-offs. Waiting or leaving it to each person lets skills drift.`, task:`L16`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which content BEST belongs in a job description for a new program lead role?`, opts:[`Purpose, duties, decision rights, and limits`,`The person's past performance reviews`,`A list of every project the person built`,`The team's dress code and attendance rules`], a:0, why:`A job description defines the role, not the person: its purpose, duties, decision rights, reporting line, required competencies, and what it doesn't own.`, task:`L21`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The only engineer who understands the interface engine plans to retire in three years. What is the BEST action?`, opts:[`Post the job now so a replacement can shadow him`,`Name one successor and hand off everything in July`,`Write a plan with named successors and skill dates`,`Keep it informal, since he's trusted and capable`], a:2, why:`A succession plan names successors, sets readiness for each skill, closes gaps with training, and is built with the incumbent. One successor or a quiet posting just moves the risk.`, task:`L21`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`An IT operating budget was $2,400,000 and actual spending was $2,580,000. What is the variance?`, opts:[`$180,000 favorable, 7.5 percent`,`$180,000 unfavorable, 6.98 percent`,`$180,000 favorable, 6.98 percent`,`$180,000 unfavorable, 7.5 percent`], a:3, why:`Variance = budget minus actual = 2,400,000 minus 2,580,000 = minus 180,000, unfavorable for an expense. Percent of budget: 180,000 / 2,400,000 = 7.5 percent. 6.98 divides by actual instead of budget.`, task:`L25`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A community health center asks to be hosted on a hospital system's EHR. What makes it MOST different from the system's own clinics?`, opts:[`It sees only patients who have no insurance`,`It has its own board and a sliding fee scale`,`It must provide inpatient and emergency care`,`It is paid by Medicare on its reasonable costs`], a:1, why:`A community health center is its own organization, often federally funded, serving anyone regardless of ability to pay on a sliding fee scale. Inpatient care and cost-based Medicare payment describe a critical access hospital.`, task:`E1`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A vendor's sepsis model reports an AUC of 0.87. Run silently, it scores 0.85 at one hospital and 0.68 at another. What should the organization do?`, opts:[`Go live everywhere, since the average is strong`,`Use the vendor's threshold and its 0.87 result`,`Go live where it performs, and keep watching it`,`Keep it silent everywhere for another full year`], a:2, why:`Judge a model on local data, site by site. Go live where it performs, with thresholds set from your own results, keep it silent where it doesn't, and monitor for drift.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`In vulnerability management, what is information security's role?`, opts:[`Patch every system in the organization itself`,`Find and rate flaws, set standards, track fixes`,`Approve the budget for every remediation project`,`Sign off on clinical workflows after each fix`], a:1, why:`Security finds and rates vulnerabilities, sets the standards and deadlines, and tracks fixes to closure. Each system's owner is accountable for remediation, and IT operations applies fixes through change control. Security doesn't patch everything itself, fund it, or approve clinical workflows.`, task:`SE5`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A year after go-live, the benefits review shows claim denials down 4 percent against a target of 10. What should happen next?`, opts:[`The owner finds the cause and sets a new review`,`Close the plan out, since go-live is complete`,`Lower the target so it matches the 4 percent`,`Ask the vendor to explain the shortfall to IT`], a:0, why:`Benefits realization keeps going after go-live. The business owner finds why the benefit fell short, acts on it, and sets the next review date. Closing the plan, moving the target, or handing it to the vendor stops tracking the benefit the money was spent for.`, task:`SA9`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which control MOST directly keeps patient information out of public AI tools?`, opts:[`A yearly training module on the safe use of AI`,`Data classification enforced by loss prevention`,`A memo from the CIO reminding staff of the rules`,`Trust in each clinician's professional judgment`], a:1, why:`Data management controls start with classification. Patient data is restricted, and restricted data gets protection a system enforces, such as data loss prevention on managed devices and networks, plus an approved AI tool under a BAA so people don't need a workaround. Training, memos, and judgment help, but none of them stops a paste. On personal phones the policy and the approved tool carry the weight.`, task:`SE6`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A three-year IT plan adds modules every year, and staff turnover is 15 percent a year. Which education strategy is BEST?`, opts:[`Repeat the go-live class every year for all staff`,`Send all staff a tip sheet with every new release`,`Train only when ticket volumes spike on a topic`,`Role-based training before access, refreshers`], a:3, why:`A standing education strategy trains new hires by role before they get access, refreshes superusers before each release, checks proficiency, and uses ticket topics to aim refreshers. A yearly repeat spends time on what people know, tip sheets alone don't build skill, and waiting for tickets trains after the errors.`, task:`L15`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which role leads nursing informatics for a health system, the way the CMIO leads it for physicians?`, opts:[`Chief nursing officer`,`Chief information security officer`,`Nurse manager of the largest unit`,`Chief nursing information officer`], a:3, why:`The chief nursing information officer, the CNIO, leads nursing informatics: nursing's voice in design, content, and optimization. The chief nursing officer leads nursing practice and staff, the CISO leads security, and a unit manager runs one unit.`, task:`E3`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `The board went into executive session at 3:40. Maria waited in the hallway outside with Aisha and Glenn, the three of them standing by the window with nothing to do, which none of them was good at.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"September,"</span> Aisha said finally. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"If Bev's numbers hold, I get my money."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"If they hold,"</span> Glenn said. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"I wrote the gate. I'll be the first one to open it."</span> Aisha looked at him like she was deciding whether that was a joke. It wasn't, and she seemed to like that better.`,
    `At 4:15 the board chair came out. The board had named Maria Santos interim chief information officer, starting Monday, April 3. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Interim,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="man">"For a year. After what happened at Rawlins, the audit committee will want to see what you do with security and privacy. Then we'll talk about the rest."</span> He shook her hand. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Ray says you'll tell us the truth early. Do that."</span>`,
    `Ochoa walked her to her car, the way he had in Greeley the day the survey ended. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Sundays,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Seven thirty. Bring the legal pad. I'll still make you write."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Who pays?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Dry, warm.">"The one with the job."</span>`,
    `She called Irene from the car. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Interim,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"For a year."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Gruff, warm.">"Did you write it for the night shift?"</span>`,
    (s.score>=30
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I tried."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Dry, sure.">"Then they'll tell you."</span>`
      :`<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I tried. Some of it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Dry, sure.">"Then they'll tell you which part."</span>`),
    ((G.tookthetickets)
      ?`The next week the board secretary sent the conflict of interest form every officer signs. Under gifts from vendors in the last two years, Maria wrote: Coors Field suite, April 2027, Corvane, during the selection. It was one line. She knew she'd be asked about it, and she was.`
      :`The next week the board secretary sent the conflict of interest form every officer signs. Under gifts from vendors in the last two years, Maria wrote none, and attached Pam's log, which said declined and disclosed, April 2027. It was the easiest page in the stack.`),
    `Friday, March 31, Ochoa's last day, there was no party. He'd said no to one. At five he walked out the front door with one box, like anybody else.`,
    `Monday morning, Maria Santos had been the interim CIO for forty minutes when the letter arrived.`,
    `The office was at the far end of the fourth floor hall. The nameplate still said the last person's name. On the windowsill was a small jade plant that hadn't been there before, with an index card in front of it in Ochoa's handwriting. Water it every other Sunday. R.O.`,
    `The letter had come in an envelope, handwritten, addressed to the CIO, and the mailroom had sent it up.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Letter from a Rawlins night nurse">Dear Ms. Santos,<br>I've been a night nurse on the medical floor in Rawlins for nineteen years. Somebody told me you worked nights in Greeley, so you'll know what I mean. For most of those years, at three in the morning, I was the only one awake who could check anything. Last Tuesday a man on my floor went bad at ten after three. A nurse in Greeley saw his pressure drop on her screen and called me before the monitor alarmed. A pharmacist in Cheyenne checked his antibiotic before I hung it. When we sent him to Greeley, I didn't fax anything. They opened his chart, and it was all there, every page. I don't know how much of that was you. My charge nurse says some of it. I wanted somebody to know I'm not the only one awake anymore.<br>Connie, RN, nights</prop>`,
    `Maria read it twice. Then she took a picture of it and sent it to Ochoa, with one line: <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I assume you've seen this."</span> He replied in under a minute. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"I haven't. It's why you have that office. Sunday, 7:30. Bring it."</span>`,
    `Her phone buzzed again. Lucia, who was supposed to be in pharmacology. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"how's the bigger office. do u have a plant yet"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Ochoa left me one,"</span> she typed. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I have instructions."</span>`,
    `The next one took longer. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"fall rotations posted. ICU. greeley. 4th floor."</span>`,
    `Maria read the last line of the letter again. I'm not the only one awake anymore. Then she took a fresh legal pad out of the desk drawer, wrote 2030 at the top, and picked up her phone.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, warm, sure.">"That's a date,"</span> she typed.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch12.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`}
});
